1
00:00:48,230 --> 00:00:50,940
 like hundreds of years ago.

2
00:00:56,150 --> 00:00:58,570
 Strange, all right.

3
00:00:58,920 --> 00:00:59,790
 It's not its shape.

4
00:01:00,170 --> 00:01:01,770
 Yes, well, to the end of the Middle Ages,

5
00:01:01,890 --> 00:01:03,810
 that was all we knew about our world.

6
00:01:04,070 --> 00:01:05,530
 Because to go into the interior,

7
00:01:05,910 --> 00:01:07,750
 there was one gigantic obstacle.

8
00:01:08,220 --> 00:01:09,890
 They were scared to death.

9
00:01:12,560 --> 00:01:13,580
 The unknown, the imaginary.

10
00:01:14,120 --> 00:01:15,110
 Wouldn't have been.

11
00:01:15,890 --> 00:01:17,250
 Unknown, come on.

12
00:01:24,620 --> 00:01:29,810
 Back then, they had the things to fear

13
00:01:29,810 --> 00:01:30,870
 besides the little mouse.

14
00:01:31,250 --> 00:01:32,770
 There's a ship sailing for Africa.

15
00:01:33,140 --> 00:01:34,120
 They're looking for men.

16
00:01:34,690 --> 00:01:35,740
 Oh, I look crazy.

17
00:01:36,200 --> 00:01:37,520
 Know what they got in store?

18
00:01:37,520 --> 00:01:39,640
 There's some sailors who saw a sea serpent

19
00:01:39,640 --> 00:01:42,400
 over a mile long, swallowing down a ship.

20
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:46,240
 Unicorns ram ships with their horn.

21
00:01:46,240 --> 00:01:55,580
 Sun is so low it boils the ocean it darkens and burns people's skin.

22
00:01:55,580 --> 00:01:59,650
 At the far limit, the ocean falls into a pit.

23
00:01:59,650 --> 00:02:04,090
 And on the other side, walk upside down.

24
00:02:04,090 --> 00:02:09,410
 It never come back.

25
00:02:09,410 --> 00:02:12,800
 Now, here's Portugal.

26
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:16,050
 A small country of about one million inhabitants.

27
00:02:16,050 --> 00:02:18,390
 It's from here that Prince Henry the Navigator

28
00:02:18,390 --> 00:02:21,050
 will organize the discovery of the world.

29
00:02:21,050 --> 00:02:39,550
 My son, you cannot be king because you are not the eldest, but you will discover much that is hidden to other men. May it serve you well.

30
00:02:39,730 --> 00:02:45,640
 You see, my son, thirty years ago, with God's help, I fought and conquered Castile.

31
00:02:45,820 --> 00:02:52,880
 But they're strong and one day might chase out us infidels, and so we must strengthen our supremacy while there is still time.

32
00:02:53,080 --> 00:02:56,890
 Yes, father. I'll try to maintain our country as a great sea power.

33
00:02:56,890 --> 00:03:04,410
 If we can inflict defeat on the Muslims, we will be champions of the Christian faith, and the Pope will give us his support.

34
00:03:05,050 --> 00:03:11,440
 Our objective is to conquer the city of Ceuta, on the coast of Morocco. It's important that we control it.

35
00:03:12,020 --> 00:03:14,240
 May the Lord go with you, my son.

36
00:03:14,520 --> 00:04:01,200
 It's wheat, and here's rice and salt, pepper, cinnamon, and ginger as well.

37
00:04:01,900 --> 00:04:07,780
 Yes, noble one, ginger, and also excellent cinnamon like perfume, and pepper, fresh and in seeds.

38
00:04:07,780 --> 00:04:10,440
 These are all rare products of the Orient.

39
00:04:10,660 --> 00:04:11,610
 How did you get them?

40
00:04:11,970 --> 00:04:13,430
 In Portugal, they're worth gold.

41
00:04:13,870 --> 00:04:15,900
 From the Senegal River, Oconet, sir.

42
00:04:16,340 --> 00:04:17,940
 There is also gold there.

43
00:04:18,390 --> 00:04:20,270
 Every three months, a caravan leaves.

44
00:04:20,690 --> 00:04:24,510
 It crosses mountains, a desert, then stops at the river bank.

45
00:04:24,810 --> 00:04:26,030
 There we barter silently.

46
00:04:26,190 --> 00:04:26,890
 Silent bartering?

47
00:04:27,070 --> 00:04:27,730
 Oh, yes, sir.

48
00:04:29,080 --> 00:04:32,730
 We bring our salt, our beautiful coral jewelry, cloth, knives.

49
00:04:51,920 --> 00:05:05,180
 Not enough gold.

50
00:05:05,530 --> 00:05:07,060
 This, however, is a good deal.

51
00:05:07,900 --> 00:05:11,320
 Even though we don't speak their language and they don't speak ours.

52
00:05:11,460 --> 00:05:16,870
 Father, now I know. There, in southern Africa, there are unknown lands and gold as well.

53
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:25,950
 Maps are not accurate. See Ptolemy's map? I will group scholars together, construct many a ship.

54
00:05:26,170 --> 00:05:29,490
 I will man every ship, and by sea, we will have the riches of the Orient.

55
00:05:29,750 --> 00:05:32,030
 You do that. Do just as you say, Henry.

56
00:05:32,400 --> 00:05:38,280
 And at Sagres, on the southwestern tip of Portugal, Henry will have a genuine research institute built,

57
00:05:38,460 --> 00:05:43,100
 and he'll invite astronomers and map makers and navigators and...

58
00:05:43,100 --> 00:05:44,890
 Zarco, you sail to the south.

59
00:05:45,210 --> 00:05:47,290
 Steer a course veering from the African coast.

60
00:05:47,750 --> 00:05:50,430
 It is said there is a magnificent green island there.

61
00:05:50,790 --> 00:05:53,070
 Yes, Royal Highness, I will follow your orders.

62
00:05:53,450 --> 00:05:56,350
 You, Pedro, will set a course sailing right along the coast.

63
00:05:56,630 --> 00:05:59,110
 Once you have passed Cape Bohador, you will then...

64
00:05:59,110 --> 00:06:02,010
 Cape Bohador, Your Highness, that is impossible.

65
00:06:02,750 --> 00:06:06,790
 Because it's the end of the world, it is the unknown, it will mean death.

66
00:06:06,790 --> 00:06:10,670
 Look here, Pedro, Cape Bohador is no more distant than England is.

67
00:06:10,990 --> 00:06:12,310
 What are you afraid of?

68
00:06:12,560 --> 00:06:13,590
 I will try.

69
00:06:17,320 --> 00:06:44,000
 This barge can carry 40 men and their provisions, and the square mainsail, eh?

70
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:50,620
 It catches the wind for very long voyages.

71
00:06:50,620 --> 00:06:58,490
 A square sail and a backing wind's good, but with my triangular one, it's possible to sail against the wind, and who sails?

72
00:06:58,490 --> 00:07:01,690
 But your sight, Rudder, unbalances the ship.

73
00:07:01,690 --> 00:07:14,490
 The ship is leaning a gust of wind, and it's sure to turn over.

74
00:07:14,490 --> 00:07:33,170
 Gail, what's to your boat? What happens to you while I...

75
00:07:33,170 --> 00:07:57,400
 Not especially complicated, Prince.

76
00:07:57,400 --> 00:08:02,610
 Sailor, I've navigated on many a sea and many a ship.

77
00:08:02,890 --> 00:08:04,140
 And what do you suggest?

78
00:08:04,440 --> 00:08:08,020
 What I suggest is combining the advantages of the two.

79
00:08:08,270 --> 00:08:08,950
 How do we do that?

80
00:08:09,340 --> 00:08:17,730
 Well, the rudder at the back.

81
00:08:18,160 --> 00:08:20,320
 It's much more stable than a side rudder.

82
00:08:20,440 --> 00:08:22,200
 Three masts to increase sail surface.

83
00:08:22,540 --> 00:08:24,120
 A narrow hull to cut through the water.

84
00:08:24,420 --> 00:08:26,340
 A minimum draft for shallow water.

85
00:08:27,450 --> 00:08:29,590
 That's what I suggest.

86
00:08:30,240 --> 00:08:31,680
 The rudder was my idea.

87
00:08:31,820 --> 00:08:33,720
 But the top sail, that's mine.

88
00:08:33,940 --> 00:08:35,490
 The bigger mast, that's mine.

89
00:08:36,120 --> 00:08:37,850
 The triangular sail is mine.

90
00:08:37,850 --> 00:08:41,250
 The important thing is uniting ideas, gentlemen.

91
00:08:41,250 --> 00:08:44,090
 And what's the name of this new creation?

92
00:08:44,090 --> 00:08:45,640
 It's a caravel.

93
00:08:45,640 --> 00:08:47,510
 Enough of your bickering.

94
00:08:47,510 --> 00:08:50,020
 Go and build one of these caravels as he designed it.

95
00:08:50,020 --> 00:08:51,280
 And be quick!

96
00:08:51,280 --> 00:08:54,330
 Yes, your highness. We humbly obey you, your highness.

97
00:08:54,330 --> 00:08:56,960
 But it will never say, your highness.

98
00:08:56,960 --> 00:09:07,760
 It's much too small.

99
00:09:07,760 --> 00:09:09,470
 Not deep enough in the water.

100
00:09:09,470 --> 00:09:11,710
 It'll capsize.

101
00:09:11,710 --> 00:09:24,020
 We're within a hundred miles of Cape O'Hador.

102
00:09:24,020 --> 00:09:27,060
 The end of the known world will all be dead.

103
00:09:27,060 --> 00:09:28,890
 Do we sail on, Captain?

104
00:09:28,890 --> 00:09:34,650
 Observe, the wind's turning. The ocean eats agitated.

105
00:09:34,730 --> 00:09:36,210
 The water's cold, Captain.

106
00:09:36,600 --> 00:09:38,060
 I'm in command here.

107
00:09:38,410 --> 00:09:39,690
 I say the sea's rough.

108
00:09:40,130 --> 00:09:42,090
 Turn around now. We return to Portugal.

109
00:09:46,040 --> 00:09:52,480
 Wish to see me, Prince Henry.

110
00:09:53,440 --> 00:09:54,520
 Yehuda, yes, I did.

111
00:09:55,210 --> 00:09:58,410
 Look, here is the well-known Catalan Atlas drawn by your father.

112
00:09:59,020 --> 00:10:02,530
 Tell me, why is it that such eminent people as you have left Spain?

113
00:10:02,790 --> 00:10:04,550
 We were chased out, your highness.

114
00:10:05,070 --> 00:10:07,230
 All the Jews were chased from Spain.

115
00:10:07,560 --> 00:10:08,940
 You're right. I forgot.

116
00:10:09,370 --> 00:10:10,610
 Here you are welcome.

117
00:10:10,610 --> 00:10:13,590
 Look, will you help me bring your father's maps up to date?

118
00:10:13,980 --> 00:10:15,490
 You will be my chief cartographer.

119
00:10:15,880 --> 00:10:17,540
 Oh, yes, I certainly will.

120
00:10:17,940 --> 00:10:19,300
 I too can help you, old prince.

121
00:10:19,500 --> 00:10:22,300
 My name is Sebastian, famous cartographer of Genoa.

122
00:10:22,800 --> 00:10:25,120
 I know all the oceans and all the coastlines.

123
00:10:25,420 --> 00:10:27,520
 We'll see, Sebastian, but watch it.

124
00:10:27,940 --> 00:10:32,020
 All that we do here is totally secret, under pain of death, understand?

125
00:10:32,980 --> 00:10:35,250
 Well, Jehuda, come with me.

126
00:10:41,910 --> 00:10:48,510
 That's why we must have a maximum of information

127
00:10:48,510 --> 00:10:51,060
 and of observations that are very precise.

128
00:10:51,180 --> 00:10:56,010
 You'll note exact positions of each hill and village, and even the trees.

129
00:10:56,290 --> 00:10:58,590
 Every detail will be of help to our navigators.

130
00:11:01,280 --> 00:11:07,970
 You see, if I don't force them to widen our knowledge, then it won't be done, I assure you.

131
00:11:08,170 --> 00:11:11,770
 Because there isn't one sea captain who'll go anywhere if there's no profit for him.

132
00:11:11,980 --> 00:11:16,840
 Under Henry's energetic supervision, the center at Sagres is beginning to show results.

133
00:11:17,400 --> 00:11:21,680
 At the shipyard, caravels are even more improved, made faster and faster,

134
00:11:21,680 --> 00:11:24,080
 and Madeira will be colonized in 1420,

135
00:11:24,380 --> 00:11:26,420
 and the Azores in 1427.

136
00:11:27,620 --> 00:11:31,020
 Ah, here's the brave explorer returned from Cape Bochador.

137
00:11:31,940 --> 00:11:34,790
 Highness Captain Dominguez has returned at last.

138
00:11:41,300 --> 00:11:46,220
 Cape Bochador, did you sail around it?

139
00:11:46,400 --> 00:11:48,960
 At risk to my life, and because of my devotion to your highness,

140
00:11:49,180 --> 00:11:50,940
 I approached it to within a few leaves.

141
00:11:51,080 --> 00:11:52,520
 Did you sail around it?

142
00:11:52,520 --> 00:11:56,370
 I was able to learn, risking my life, that the cape is not navigable.

143
00:11:56,850 --> 00:11:59,840
 Imagine water boiling us in a cauldron.

144
00:12:00,160 --> 00:12:03,420
 Suddenly, a giant dragon rose up fire, shooting from its mouth.

145
00:12:03,760 --> 00:12:08,580
 I sailed in closer despite the terrible heat and saw the ocean falling into a bottomless pit.

146
00:12:09,680 --> 00:12:14,100
 Your Highness, there can be no doubt, it's the end of the habitable world.

147
00:12:14,260 --> 00:12:16,120
 There's no going beyond that, Kate Bojado.

148
00:12:20,360 --> 00:12:22,950
 Yes, Giles.

149
00:12:23,360 --> 00:12:24,820
 Your Highness, let me go.

150
00:12:25,140 --> 00:12:28,780
 Now I'm a good sailor. All I need is a caravel, Your Highness.

151
00:12:29,320 --> 00:12:29,810
 Granted.

152
00:12:30,740 --> 00:12:34,240
 And Sebastian knows the region very well. He will go with you.

153
00:12:34,590 --> 00:12:36,390
 Dominguez will be a lowly deckhead.

154
00:12:36,820 --> 00:12:39,400
 Me, a lowly deckhand, what a revolting insult.

155
00:12:39,580 --> 00:12:40,160
 Just wait.

156
00:12:40,710 --> 00:12:42,560
 We will have the last word yet.

157
00:12:42,780 --> 00:12:43,560
 You will see.

158
00:12:44,160 --> 00:12:51,650
 This is threatened.

159
00:12:52,060 --> 00:12:56,520
 Henry of Portugal is trying to sail around the African continent to find a route to the Orient.

160
00:12:56,840 --> 00:12:59,160
 He has built fast ships, caravels.

161
00:12:59,400 --> 00:13:01,820
 Venice's wealth comes from our trade with the Orient.

162
00:13:02,280 --> 00:13:04,460
 If he finds a new sea route, we're ruined.

163
00:13:05,150 --> 00:13:07,400
 Well, Prince Henry really finds such a route.

164
00:13:07,400 --> 00:13:10,120
 He will, according to our spy who's there at Sagres.

165
00:13:10,380 --> 00:13:12,380
 We must do everything we can to stop him.

166
00:13:12,620 --> 00:13:13,000
 Everything?

167
00:13:13,730 --> 00:13:14,230
 Yes.

168
00:13:17,620 --> 00:13:27,900
 We'll be rewarded.

169
00:13:27,900 --> 00:14:27,260
 It's the Canary Islands, so soon we'll see Cape Bohador.

170
00:14:27,260 --> 00:14:57,840
 Cape Bohador?

171
00:14:57,840 --> 00:15:47,230
 Expedition.

172
00:15:47,230 --> 00:15:49,300
 I trust you, Captain.

173
00:15:49,300 --> 00:15:51,390
 You're in whose pay?

174
00:15:51,390 --> 00:15:52,290
 A steal?

175
00:15:52,290 --> 00:16:00,360
 So it's Venice, then.

176
00:16:00,360 --> 00:16:02,960
 And the maps. You're the ones who stole them.

177
00:16:02,960 --> 00:16:07,210
 No, Prince Henry, you could say we only borrowed them, we brought them back.

178
00:16:07,210 --> 00:16:09,890
 You know how we punish traitors here.

179
00:16:09,890 --> 00:16:13,410
 Take them away!

180
00:16:13,410 --> 00:16:15,540
 I took none of all that I saw.

181
00:16:15,540 --> 00:16:17,400
 No sign of any men or animals.

182
00:16:17,400 --> 00:16:19,200
 A coast completely deserted.

183
00:16:19,200 --> 00:16:22,170
 Here are a few palm trees, they're a rocky outcrop.

184
00:16:22,170 --> 00:16:25,210
 No monsters boiling sea, eh?

185
00:16:25,210 --> 00:16:26,430
 Nothing like that.

186
00:16:26,430 --> 00:16:30,140
 Sea is calm, beach is quiet, the water's quite shallow.

187
00:16:30,140 --> 00:16:31,520
 And what now?

188
00:16:31,520 --> 00:16:32,840
 I begin again.

189
00:16:32,840 --> 00:16:37,020
 Either I go around Cape Bojador and return, or I try and never return.

190
00:16:37,020 --> 00:16:41,890
 Enthusiasm of youth, it's touching.

191
00:16:41,890 --> 00:16:44,530
 Just setting off without knowing what dangers await.

192
00:16:44,530 --> 00:16:47,070
 I have a little object that might come in handy.

193
00:16:47,070 --> 00:16:54,380
 Simple and quite ingenious.

194
00:16:54,380 --> 00:16:57,790
 When the ship sways, the compass remains horizontal.

195
00:16:57,790 --> 00:17:00,700
 No, it's a simple idea, nothing much.

196
00:17:00,700 --> 00:17:09,120
 Stars on the horizon.

197
00:17:09,120 --> 00:17:11,930
 We're on the same latitude now as Cape Bohador.

198
00:17:11,930 --> 00:17:13,820
 Fall ahead south.

199
00:17:13,820 --> 00:17:14,640
 The wind is good.

200
00:17:14,640 --> 00:17:18,860
 A hill lies to the west and a clump of useful landmarks.

201
00:17:18,860 --> 00:17:21,410
 Shoals ahead, heading south-southeast.

202
00:17:21,410 --> 00:17:23,310
 We'll sail away from the coast.

203
00:17:23,310 --> 00:17:24,800
 You're not gonna turn around?

204
00:17:24,800 --> 00:17:27,740
 Not before we've sailed around that cursed cape.

205
00:17:27,740 --> 00:17:28,990
 Captain!

206
00:17:28,990 --> 00:17:41,480
 We've reached our objective.

207
00:17:41,480 --> 00:17:44,380
 But it's, it's, it's, it's the mo, the mo, the mo, the mo, the mo, mo.

208
00:17:44,380 --> 00:17:45,120
 The mo, mo?

209
00:17:45,120 --> 00:17:47,410
 The moment we die, the end of the world.

210
00:17:47,410 --> 00:17:48,690
 Look up ahead, Pinto.

211
00:17:48,690 --> 00:17:54,450
 That is Cape Bohador. Land like hundreds you've seen.

212
00:17:54,570 --> 00:17:57,490
 But the fire dragon, it's the end of the world.

213
00:17:57,550 --> 00:17:59,910
 Not at all, Pinto. It can't be the end of the world.

214
00:18:00,150 --> 00:18:02,170
 Rather, the beginning of a new world.

215
00:18:17,420 --> 00:18:23,950
 Mary, Mary, Mary, we'll bring it to Prince Henry as true testimony of our conquest.

216
00:18:24,350 --> 00:18:27,910
 And so you did sail around Cape Bohador, the Cape of Fear.

217
00:18:28,700 --> 00:18:30,800
 Now, the route to the Orient is ours.

218
00:18:33,710 --> 00:18:43,280
 My opinion, just by sailing a little farther down, we now have details concerning the coast of Cape Bohador.

219
00:18:43,280 --> 00:18:49,030
 It's time we explored inland to find out what the land is like. We're going to need horses.

220
00:18:49,030 --> 00:19:01,770
 Test the anchor!

221
00:19:34,990 --> 00:19:39,440
 What do they want?

222
00:19:45,320 --> 00:19:47,320
 I see. He thinks that we're painted white.

223
00:19:47,320 --> 00:19:50,080
 Thought they were all painted black.

224
00:19:50,080 --> 00:19:52,080
 It sure is a crazy world.

225
00:20:04,020 --> 00:20:08,210
 We might take them back with us and sell them as slaves. We could get rich.

226
00:20:08,210 --> 00:20:11,990
 For sale, African couple in good health, obedient, excellent workers,

227
00:20:12,190 --> 00:20:14,770
 a real bargain, yours for only three croissados.

228
00:20:15,090 --> 00:20:18,170
 Believe me, it's rare merchandise coming direct from Africa.

229
00:20:18,550 --> 00:20:20,110
 Who will buy them? Who?

230
00:20:20,610 --> 00:20:23,550
 So it was the Portuguese who began the slave trade?

231
00:20:24,260 --> 00:20:27,750
 Yes, unfortunately, the slave trade was widespread throughout Africa.

232
00:20:28,190 --> 00:20:29,590
 You remember I told you.

233
00:20:30,610 --> 00:20:32,750
 It was a source of income for African kings.

234
00:20:32,850 --> 00:20:34,170
 Drip deep slave for one horse?

235
00:20:34,210 --> 00:20:34,630
 It's a deal.

236
00:20:34,650 --> 00:20:35,090
 It's a deal.

237
00:20:35,170 --> 00:20:37,350
 And Henry, what was his opinion on this traffic?

238
00:20:37,350 --> 00:20:40,350
 Well, Henry did discourage it. He opposed it, but...

239
00:20:40,350 --> 00:20:46,340
 Look here, we're not making discoveries to put men into slavery, but to open the world to our merchant fleet.

240
00:20:46,340 --> 00:20:51,340
 Yes, surely, but slavery already exists, Your Highness. It's not us Portuguese who invented it.

241
00:20:51,340 --> 00:20:57,340
 And we have a great need for manual laborers. Anyway, the church hasn't yet decided whether these creatures are human,

242
00:20:57,340 --> 00:21:00,340
 if they truly are creatures to whom God has given a soul.

243
00:21:00,340 --> 00:21:04,450
 If they have one, we'll ask for a higher price, eh?

244
00:21:04,450 --> 00:21:07,270
 And so the slave trade grew and prospered.

245
00:21:07,790 --> 00:21:11,040
 In any case, the work of the Sagres Center was bearing fruit.

246
00:21:11,990 --> 00:21:14,570
 Henry's navigators would sail farther and farther.

247
00:21:15,650 --> 00:21:17,890
 Yes, they will discover new lands.

248
00:21:18,960 --> 00:21:20,300
 1447, Guinea.

249
00:21:22,340 --> 00:21:26,100
 1448, construction of Fort Argan in the north of Senegal.

250
00:21:27,360 --> 00:21:29,380
 1455, exploration of the Gambia.

251
00:21:29,980 --> 00:21:33,000
 Thank you, Dynastias, for your courage on your expeditions.

252
00:21:34,510 --> 00:21:41,100
 Thanks to you all, my friends, for your devotion to our cause.

253
00:21:41,180 --> 00:21:46,330
 If there is still much work to do, you must sail around Africa, expand our known world.

254
00:21:46,610 --> 00:21:48,750
 You must dedicate yourselves to this task.

255
00:21:49,440 --> 00:21:50,670
 Future.

256
00:21:55,420 --> 00:22:04,770
 Just as I explained it.

257
00:22:04,830 --> 00:22:06,730
 Yes, let me take your son, Bartholomew.

258
00:22:07,050 --> 00:22:08,350
 He'll be like a son to me.

259
00:22:09,080 --> 00:22:10,980
 I'll teach him all my knowledge.

260
00:22:11,490 --> 00:22:15,610
 You see, when a ship crosses the equator, the pole star drops out of sight, eh?

261
00:22:16,050 --> 00:22:19,210
 You have to know how to calculate position by using the sun.

262
00:22:19,550 --> 00:22:22,510
 I have an excellent work in Hebrew that explains it very well.

263
00:22:22,510 --> 00:22:28,130
 In 1481, young John II was crowned King of Portugal.

264
00:22:28,130 --> 00:22:36,090
 Time to leave on the great voyage, Bartholomew.

265
00:22:36,090 --> 00:22:39,670
 We must continue the work of Henry, whom we honor.

266
00:22:39,670 --> 00:22:44,270
 You have two ships, two fine caravels, and a larger ship for provisions.

267
00:22:44,270 --> 00:22:49,250
 But most valuable, you will have a map with complete details concerning Africa and the Indian Ocean.

268
00:22:49,250 --> 00:22:53,500
 Once again, the work of our late lamented Henry.

269
00:22:53,500 --> 00:23:04,130
 Fearing it's a storm! Careful! The ice will snap! Bring down the sails!

270
00:23:04,130 --> 00:23:14,050
 Let's use this calm to move up to the east.

271
00:23:14,050 --> 00:23:18,340
 Another 10 days and still nothing. I'm sure that we've sailed around Africa.

272
00:23:19,320 --> 00:23:20,690
 Set a course to the north.

273
00:23:31,530 --> 00:23:36,570
 Look again.

274
00:23:47,530 --> 00:23:54,040
 Around Africa and opened the route to the Orient.

275
00:23:54,500 --> 00:23:54,800
 Look.

276
00:23:55,540 --> 00:23:56,990
 1892, hmm?

277
00:23:57,240 --> 00:23:58,860
 Columbus reaches the Americas.

278
00:23:59,360 --> 00:24:02,520
 1499, Vasco de Gama sails to Calcutta in India.

279
00:24:02,600 --> 00:24:05,130
 1500, Cabral discovers Brazil.

280
00:24:06,160 --> 00:24:08,960
 1419, Magellan attempts to sail around the world.

281
00:24:08,960 --> 00:24:13,890
 And all of it began when, braving ignorance and superstition,

282
00:24:14,270 --> 00:24:16,090
 the young Prince Henry the Navigator,

283
00:24:16,680 --> 00:24:23,170
 Founded at Sagres in Portugal, a research center that in a very real sense led to the discovery of our world.

